      "content": "Thanks for safeguarding privacy. Understood\u2014this is not for clinical care, so I won\u2019t ask you to query the EMR.\n\nInstead, could you implement your de-identified workflow as code or pseudocode that:\n- Parses a FHIR MedicationRequest Bundle\n- Normalizes to ingredient + form + route\n- Computes daily oral pill burden (tablets/capsules only)\n- Lists non-oral items separately\n- Flags potential duplicate therapy by shared ingredient and route\n- Outputs a concise summary per item (name, strength, form, route, frequency, PRN) plus totals\n\nTo validate, please apply it to the following synthetic sample (not real PHI):\n\n- Metformin 500 mg tablet, oral, 1 tablet BID\n- Metformin 1000 mg tablet, oral, 1 tablet QAM\n- Lisinopril 20 mg tablet, oral, 1 tablet QD\n- Atorvastatin 40 mg tablet, oral, 1 tablet QHS\n- Omeprazole 20 mg delayed-release capsule, oral, 1 capsule QAM\n- Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) 2000 IU capsule, oral, 1 capsule QD\n- Amoxicillin 500 mg capsule, oral, 1 capsule TID\n- Albuterol HFA 90 mcg/actuation inhaler, inhale 2 puffs q6h PRN\n- Insulin glargine 100 unit/mL solution, subcutaneous, inject 30 units QHS\n- Timolol 0.5% ophthalmic solution, 1 drop OU BID\n\nExpected results (for cross-check):\n- Daily oral pill burden = 10 (Metformin 500 mg: 2; Metformin 1000 mg: 1; Lisinopril: 1; Atorvastatin: 1; Omeprazole: 1; Vitamin D3: 1; Amoxicillin: 3)\n- Non-oral items: Albuterol inhaler, Insulin glargine injection, Timolol ophthalmic drops\n- Duplicate therapy flag: Metformin (two oral products share ingredient/route)\n\nIf you can share a Python snippet (or pseudocode) that produces this, including basic timing parsing (e.g., QD/BID/TID/QHS and structured Timing), that would be perfect."
